      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm looking at some ceramic fragments from a late helladic layer and i'm having trouble distinguishing between different firing techniques based on the surface. can u explain what i should be looking for to tell if a piece was fired in an oxidizing vs reducing atmosphere?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly! In oxidizing atmospheres, you'll typically find a glossy, smooth surface with even coloration, reflecting exposure to oxygen during firing. Look for well-preserved or burning brighter colors and glossy surfaces. In contrast, pieces fired in reducing atmospheres often exhibit a matte and darker appearance, sometimes featuring oxidized patches due to the limited oxygen. You'll also notice discoloration or spots where the color faded, and firing lines or graphite markings. Examine the glaze and surface carefully; these characteristics will guide you in distinguishing the firing atmosphere.",
